## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to architect a complete, safety-critical computer vision pipeline operating under real-time constraints in dynamic, cluttered environments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular 3D reconstruction leveraging temporal and geometric cues
- Multi-object tracking in scenarios with frequent clutter and occlusion
- Hazard prediction from partial observations and contact-event inference
- Few-shot on-device adaptation for novel object categories
- Edge-GPU optimization: latency, memory footprint, and compute budget trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: modules and data flow
class Monocular3DSystem {
  constructor() {
    this.depthNet = new LightweightDepthEncoder();
    this.tracker = new MultiObjectTracker();
    this.hazardPredictor = new ContactEventPredictor();
    this.adaptationHead = new FewShotAdapter();
  }

  processFrame(frame, context = {}) {
    const depth = this.depthNet.infer(frame);
    const poses = this.depthNet.extractEgoMotion(depth, context.prevDepth);
    const objects = this.tracker.associate(depth, poses);
    const hazards = this.hazardPredictor.predict(objects, depth, poses);
    return { depth, poses, objects, hazards };
  }

  adapt(shots) {
    return this.adaptationHead.fineTune(shots);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Safety-critical navigation in urban environments requires reliable 3D pose estimation and intent understanding from monocular video. A new angle tests on-device adaptation to unseen clothing and backpacks, plus robust temporal reasoning under occlusion, aligning with real-world edge deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Monocular 3D pose estimation from a single RGB stream
- Pedestrian intent recognition for near-term collisions
- Edge latency constraints and adapter-based on-device learning
- Synthetic+real data strategies to cover diverse outfits and behaviors
- Temporal consistency and occlusion robustness in losses

## Code Example

```javascript
function computeLoss(outputs, targets) {
  const poseLoss = l2(outputs.pose, targets.pose);
  const intentLoss = crossEntropy(outputs.intent, targets.intent);
  const temporalLoss = smoothL1(outputs.posePrev, outputs.pose);
  return poseLoss + 0.5 * intentLoss + 0.2 * temporalLoss;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evaluate 3D pose accuracy and intent classification in crowded urban scenes?
- What mechanisms ensure adapters do not drift over successive 5-shot updates?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end CV design with 3D reconstruction, uncertainty, and fast on-device adaptation, crucial for tool manipulation in cluttered spaces.

## Key Concepts
- 3D pose + mesh prediction from monocular input
- Uncertainty estimation and active adaptation
- Edge-efficient architectures and losses for consistency

## Code Example
```javascript
function loss(params){
  const L2 = reprojLoss(params);
  const Lpose = poseLoss(params);
  const Lmesh = meshLoss(params);
  const Lreg = temporalEdgeReg(params);
  return L2 + Lpose + Lmesh + Lreg;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you calibrate uncertainty for active-learning? 
- What would you change for multiple simultaneous tools?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model non-rigid objects and integrate deformation estimation with robotic manipulation under strict latency constraints. It also probes on-device few-shot adaptation and data strategy across synthetic and real data.

## Key Concepts
- Non-rigid pose + deformation
- Photometric, silhouette, and temporal losses
- Differentiable cloth priors for realism
- Edge-optimized, multi-branch architecture
- Lightweight 5-shot on-device adaptation

## Code Example
```javascript
function forward(inputRGB) {
  const features = encoder(inputRGB);
  const pose = poseBranch(features);
  const deform = deformBranch(features);
  return { pose6DoF: pose, deformationField: deform };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to evaluate generalization to unseen fabrics and lighting
- How to robustly handle occlusions and fast motion in clutter

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design real-time non-rigid 3D reconstruction from monocular input, integrating differentiable rendering and on-device adaptation—critical for AR try-on. It also probes data strategy, evaluation, and edge-latency trade-offs in a commercial retail context.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular non-rigid 3D reconstruction with deformable garments
- Differentiable rendering and photometric consistency
- Temporal fusion for stability
- 5-shot on-device adaptation for unseen fabrics
- Edge latency and data strategy (synthetic+real)

## Code Example
```javascript
function forward(frame, params){
  const latent = encoder(frame, params.enc);
  const mesh = deformableMesh(latent, params.shape);
  const render = renderer(mesh, params.tex);
  const loss = photometric(render, frame) + temporal(mesh);
  return {mesh, loss};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle occlusion or loose fabrics?
- How evaluate mesh quality vs. latency?
- How would you construct synthetic data for textiles?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design cross-modal, real-time computer vision pipelines that integrate on-device adaptation with differentiable geometry for robotic manipulation and planning in unstructured environments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular depth and surface normals estimation from a single RGB camera
- Differentiable probabilistic mesh prediction integrated with motion planning
- Few-shot (5-shot) on-device adaptation using synthetic pre-training followed by real-world fine-tuning
- Multi-task learning with shared encoders and task-specific heads

## Code Example

```javascript
// Core inference pipeline sketch
function forwardPass(frame) {
  const features = sharedEncoder(frame);
  const depth = depthHead(features);
  const normals = normalHead(features);
  const mesh = meshHead(features);
  return { depth, normals, mesh };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you quantify uncertainty in the mesh predictions to ensure safe manipulation?
- What strategies would you use to handle reflective or texture-less surfaces where depth estimation typically fails?
- How does the system handle dynamic scene changes between adaptation and deployment?
